ypoxemia, or abnormal vitals)", "Consider influenza testing and antivirals during influenza season or in high-risk patients", "Consider asthma or COPD when wheezing or a prolonged cough is prominent"], "criteria": [], "scores": []}, "differential": [{"name": "Community-acquired pneumonia", "slug": "community-acquired-pneumonia", "why_considered": "Cough, fever, and chest symptoms overlap; the key task is to avoid missing a lung infection that needs antibiotics.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Fever of 100.4°F or higher, tachypnea, focal crackles, hypoxemia, or abnormal vitals", "discriminators": {"history": ["Higher fever with shaking chills", "Pleuritic chest pain", "Shortness of breath at rest", "Feeling more severely ill"], "exam": ["Focal crackles or bronchial breath sounds", "Dullness to percussion", "Increased tactile fremitus", "Tachypnea or hypoxemia"], "labs": ["Leukocytosis with left shift", "Elevated CRP or procalcitonin", "Hypoxemia on pulse oximetry"], "imaging": ["Focal consolidation or infiltrate on chest x-ray", "Air bronchograms or pleural effusion"]}}, {"name": "Asthma exacerbation", "slug": "asthma", "why_considered": "Wheezing, chest tightness, and cough are prominent in both; asthma may be triggered by the same viral illness.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Recurrent episodes of wheeze and cough", "Known asthma or prior inhaler use", "Symptoms triggered by allergens, exercise, or cold air", "Nighttime awakening with cough or wheeze"], "exam": ["Diffuse expiratory wheezing", "Prolonged expiratory phase", "Improvement after bronchodilator"], "labs": ["Eosinophilia may be present", "Allergy testing or IgE elevation if atopic"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray usually normal or hyperinflated"]}}, {"name": "Influenza", "slug": "influenza", "why_considered": "Cough, fever, and body aches overlap; influenza can cause bronchitis and is treatable with antivirals.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Abrupt onset of high fever, severe myalgias, and headache", "Influenza circulating in the community", "Presentation within the first few days of illness"], "exam": ["High fever", "Diffuse muscle tenderness", "Cough may be dry initially"], "labs": ["Positive rapid influenza test or PCR"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray normal unless complicated by pneumonia"]}}, {"name": "Common cold", "slug": "common-cold", "why_considered": "Both are viral upper respiratory illnesses with cough, but a cold is milder and more focused on the nose and throat.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Prominent nasal congestion and sneezing", "Sore throat early", "Cough is mild and not the main complaint", "Low-grade or no fever"], "exam": ["Nasal mucosal swelling and clear discharge", "Pharyngeal erythema without exudate", "Lungs clear to auscultation"], "labs": ["Not routinely needed"], "imaging": ["Not indicated"]}}, {"name": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbation", "slug": "copd", "why_considered": "Productive cough, wheezing, and dyspnea can occur in both, especially in smokers.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Long-standing daily cough and sputum production", "Progressive dyspnea over years", "Heavy smoking history", "Prior COPD diagnosis or inhaler use"], "exam": ["Barrel chest, prolonged expiration", "Diffuse wheezing and decreased breath sounds", "Use of accessory muscles"], "labs": ["Elevated CO2 on blood gas if severe", "Polycythemia may be present"], "imaging": ["Hyperinflation and flattened diaphragms on chest x-ray"]}}, {"name": "Pertussis", "slug": "pertussis", "why_considered": "Prolonged cough illness that can be mistaken for bronchitis, especially when cough lasts weeks.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Paroxysmal coughing fits followed by a whoop or post-tussive vomiting", "Cough lasting more than 2 weeks", "Close contact with someone with similar cough", "Incomplete vaccination"], "exam": ["Cough paroxysms during exam", "Conjunctival hemorrhage or petechiae from coughing"], "labs": ["Lymphocytosis", "Positive pertussis PCR or culture from nasopharyngeal swab"], "imaging": ["Chest x-ray usually normal"]}}, {"name": "Tuberculosis", "slug": "tuberculosis", "why_considered": "Chronic cough with sputum can mimic bronchitis when symptoms persist beyond the usual course.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Cough lasting more than 3 weeks with night sweats, weight loss, hemoptysis, or known exposure", "discriminators": {"history": ["Cough lasting weeks to months", "Night sweats, fever, weight loss", "Hemoptysis", "Born in or travel to a high-prevalence country", "Known exposure to tuberculosis"], "exam": ["Cachexia", "Cervical lymphadenopathy", "Focal crackles or cavitary breath sounds"], "labs": ["Positive interferon-gamma release assay or tuberculin skin test", "Acid-fast bacilli on sputum smear or positive culture"], "imaging": ["Upper lobe infiltrates, cavitation, or miliary pattern on chest x-ray"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Cough without fever, normal vital signs, no dyspnea, able to perform daily activities; lungs may have scattered rhonchi that clear with cough.", "moderate": "Low-grade fever, mild tachypnea, cough interfering with sleep, wheezing or chest tightness; no hypoxemia at rest.", "severe": "Fever, tachypnea, use of accessory muscles, hypoxemia (SpO2 90–94%), and significant functional impairment; consider pneumonia or another diagnosis."}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Fever of 100.4°F or higher with shaking chills", "meaning": "Suggests pneumonia or another bacterial infection rather than simple viral bronchitis"}, {"finding": "Tachypnea or hypoxemia (SpO2 < 94%)", "meaning": "Suggests pneumonia, pulmonary embolism, or another serious process"}, {"finding": "Focal crackles, dullness, or bronchial breath sounds", "meaning": "Suggests pneumonia or another focal lung process"}, {"finding": "Hemoptysis", "meaning": "Suggests pneumonia, tuberculosis, malignancy, or pulmonary embolism"}, {"finding": "Cough lasting more than 3 weeks with weight loss or night sweats", "meaning": "Suggests tuberculosis or another chronic infection"}, {"finding": "Severe dyspnea at rest or inability to speak in full sentences", "meaning": "Suggests respiratory failure or a serious alternative diagnosis"}], "complications": [{"name": "Secondary bacterial pneumonia", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["New fever after initial improvement", "Focal crackles", "Productive cough with purulent sputum", "Leukocytosis"]}, {"name": "Exacerbation of underlying asthma or COPD", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Worsening wheezing", "Increased dyspnea", "Poor response to bronchodilators"]}, {"name": "Post-tussive syncope", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Fainting immediately after a coughing fit"]}, {"name": "Rib fracture from severe coughing", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Focal chest wall pain", "Point tenderness over a rib"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Young children may not produce sputum and instead swallow it; nasal congestion, vomiting after coughing, and irritability may be more prominent. Wheezing is common."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "May present with less fever and more confusion or general weakness; cough may be less forceful and sputum production minimal. Higher risk of pneumonia."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Symptoms may be more severe and prolonged; higher risk of progression to pneumonia and atypical pathogens. Lower threshold for chest imaging and cultures."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Management focuses on supportive care; avoid unnecessary antibiotics. Influenza testing and treatment are important because pregnancy increases risk of complications."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "How long have you had the cough?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Days to about 3 weeks; under 3 weeks fits acute bronchitis, longer raises concern for pertussis, tuberculosis, or another chronic cause"}, {"question": "Did it start with a cold or sore throat?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Gradual onset a few days after an upper respiratory infection"}, {"question": "Are you coughing anything up, and what does it look like?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Clear, white, yellow, or green sputum; color alone does not prove a bacterial cause"}, {"question": "Have you had any fever, and how high did it get?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Low-grade or no fever; fever of 100.4°F or higher with chills suggests pneumonia"}, {"question": "Are you short of breath, and is it only with activity or also at rest?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "No dyspnea or only mild exertional dyspnea; dyspnea at rest suggests pneumonia or another serious process"}, {"question": "Have you coughed up any blood?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Absent; hemoptysis suggests pneumonia, tuberculosis, malignancy, or pulmonary embolism"}, {"question": "Do you have chest pain, and does it hurt more when you breathe in deeply?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Chest tightness or soreness from coughing; pleuritic pain suggests pneumonia or pulmonary embolism"}, {"question": "Any wheezing or chest tightness?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Mild wheezing may occur; prominent or recurrent wheeze suggests asthma or COPD"}, {"question": "Any night sweats, weight loss, or cough lasting weeks?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Absent; present suggests tuberculosis or another chronic infection"}, {"question": "Do you smoke, or does anyone in your home smoke?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Tobacco smoke exposure is a common trigger and risk factor"}, {"question": "Do you have asthma, COPD, or another chronic lung condition?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Underlying lung disease that may be exacerbated or that changes the differential"}, {"question": "Have you been around anyone with a similar cough, or is there illness going around at work, school, or daycare?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Community or household viral illness; whooping cough contacts if the cough is prolonged"}, {"question": "Have you been hospitalized recently or taken antibiotics in the past few months?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Recent healthcare exposure raises concern for pneumonia or resistant organisms"}, {"question": "Have you ever been exposed to tuberculosis, or were you born or have you traveled in a country where it is common?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Risk factors that would prompt testing when the cough is chronic"}], "accepted_names": ["Acute bronchitis", "Chest cold", "Viral bronchitis", "Acute viral bronchitis", "Bronchitis"], "reasoning_points": ["Cough is the dominant symptom, often productive, lasting days to a few weeks", "Onset typically follows a viral upper respiratory infection", "Low-grade or no fever, and the patient is not toxic-appearing", "Lung exam shows diffuse rhonchi or coarse crackles that clear with coughing, without focal findings", "No focal crackles, dullness, egophony, or tachypnea to suggest pneumonia", "Vital signs and oxygen saturation are reassuring", "Chest x-ray is not needed when the presentation is typical and pneumonia is not suspected", "Antibiotics are not indicated because the cause is usually viral"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Young or middle-aged adult with a cough for about a week after a cold", "Low-grade or no fever, mild sputum production", "Diffuse rhonchi that clear with coughing", "Normal vital signs and oxygen saturation"], "medium": ["Patient with asthma or COPD where wheezing and cough could be an exacerbation rather than simple bronchitis", "Fever up to 100.4°F with a mildly elevated white count, requiring a decision about chest imaging", "Older adult with a weaker cough and less fever, where pneumonia must be excluded", "Cough lasting 2 to 3 weeks, raising the question of pertussis"], "hard": ["Older adult with confusion or general weakness and minimal cough", "Immunocompromised patient with prolonged or severe symptoms and risk of atypical pathogens", "Patient with cough for more than 3 weeks plus night sweats or weight loss, suggesting tuberculosis", "Presentation with wheezing and hypoxemia that turns out to be pneumonia or an asthma exacerbation"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
