btained only to evaluate chest pain or rule out a cardiac cause of upper abdominal pain", "frequency": "common"}]}, "imaging": [{"study": "Ultrasound of the right lower quadrant", "modality": "ultrasound", "preferred_when": "Children and pregnancy (no radiation)", "findings": [{"text": "Noncompressible, blind-ending tubular structure", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Appendix diameter over 6 mm", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Appendix not seen (nondiagnostic study)", "frequency": "sometimes"}]}, {"study": "CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast", "modality": "ct", "preferred_when": "Nonpregnant adults", "findings": [{"text": "Dilated appendix over 6 mm with wall thickening and enhancement", "frequency": "almost_always"}, {"text": "Periappendiceal fat stranding", "frequency": "common"}, {"text": "Appendicolith", "frequency": "sometimes"}, {"text": "Periappendiceal fluid collection or abscess (perforation)", "frequency": "rare"}, {"text": "Extraluminal free air (perforation)", "frequency": "rare"}]}, {"study": "MRI abdomen and pelvis without contrast", "modality": "mri", "preferred_when": "Pregnancy when ultrasound is nondiagnostic", "findings": [{"text": "Dilated appendix with surrounding inflammation", "frequency": "common"}]}, {"study": "Abdominal x-ray", "modality": "xray", "findings": [{"text": "Usually nonspecific; occasionally a calcified appendicolith", "frequency": "rare"}]}], "workup": {"initial": [{"test": "cbc", "why": "Leukocytosis with left shift supports inflammation"}, {"test": "bmp", "why": "Hydration and electrolytes before surgery"}, {"test": "ua", "why": "Looks for urinary infection or a stone"}, {"test": "hcg", "why": "Rules out pregnancy and ectopic pregnancy before imaging"}, {"test": "Ultrasound of the right lower quadrant", "why": "Avoids radiation", "only_if": "child or pregnant patient"}], "confirmatory": [{"test": "CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast", "why": "Most accurate test in nonpregnant adults"}], "supporting": [{"test": "crp", "why": "Adds to the white count in scoring and suggests perforation when high"}, {"test": "MRI abdomen and pelvis without contrast", "why": "Avoids radiation", "only_if": "pregnant and ultrasound nondiagnostic"}], "not_indicated": [{"test": "Abdominal x-ray", "why": "Rarely changes management"}, {"test": "d_dimer", "why": "No role in right lower quadrant pain"}, {"test": "lipase", "why": "Not needed unless the pain is epigastric"}], "decision_points": ["Pregnancy test before CT in anyone who could be pregnant", "Ultrasound first in children and pregnancy; CT if nondiagnostic in nonpregnant patients", "Peritonitis or instability goes to surgery without waiting for imaging"], "criteria": [], "scores": ["alvarado", "pediatric_appendicitis_score"]}, "differential": [{"name": "Ectopic pregnancy", "slug": "ectopic-pregnancy", "why_considered": "Lower abdominal pain in a patient who could be pregnant; can mimic appendicitis closely and is life-threatening.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Any patient of childbearing potential with abdominal pain, especially with a missed period or vaginal bleeding.", "discriminators": {"history": ["Missed period", "Vaginal bleeding or spotting", "Pain may be sudden and severe", "Prior ectopic pregnancy or tubal surgery"], "exam": ["Adnexal tenderness or mass", "Peritoneal signs possible", "Cervical motion tenderness"], "labs": ["Positive hCG", "Falling hematocrit if bleeding"], "imaging": ["No intrauterine pregnancy on transvaginal ultrasound", "Free fluid in the pelvis", "Adnexal mass"]}}, {"name": "Ovarian torsion", "slug": "ovarian-torsion", "why_considered": "Sudden one-sided lower abdominal pain with nausea and vomiting, often in a young patient.", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Abrupt onset of severe one-sided pelvic pain with vomiting at the start.", "discriminators": {"history": ["Sudden onset, often while moving or exercising", "Vomiting at or near onset", "Known ovarian cyst"], "exam": ["Adnexal tenderness", "Unilateral pelvic pain"], "labs": ["hCG negative", "White count may be mildly elevated"], "imaging": ["Enlarged ovary with reduced or absent Doppler flow on ultrasound"]}}, {"name": "Pelvic inflammatory disease", "slug": "pelvic-inflammatory-disease", "why_considered": "Lower abdominal pain and fever in a sexually active patient.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Vaginal discharge", "New or multiple sexual partners", "Bilateral or diffuse lower pain", "Pain with intercourse"], "exam": ["Cervical motion tenderness", "Adnexal tenderness", "Mucopurulent cervical discharge"], "labs": ["Positive gonorrhea or chlamydia testing", "hCG negative"], "imaging": ["Tubal thickening or fluid on ultrasound; often normal"]}}, {"name": "Viral gastroenteritis", "slug": "gastroenteritis", "why_considered": "Nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain are common in both conditions.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Vomiting or diarrhea before the pain", "Watery diarrhea", "Sick contacts with similar illness", "Diffuse crampy pain rather than focal"], "exam": ["Diffuse mild tenderness without localization", "Active bowel sounds"], "labs": ["White count usually normal"], "imaging": ["Not routinely needed; normal appendix if imaged"]}}, {"name": "Kidney stone", "slug": "kidney-stones", "why_considered": "Right-sided abdominal or flank pain with nausea and vomiting.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Colicky flank pain radiating to the groin", "Writhing, cannot get comfortable", "Prior stone history"], "exam": ["Costovertebral angle tenderness", "No focal right lower quadrant peritoneal signs"], "labs": ["Hematuria on urinalysis"], "imaging": ["Stone and hydronephrosis on CT or ultrasound"]}}, {"name": "Mesenteric adenitis", "why_considered": "Right lower quadrant pain in a child or young adult, often after a viral illness.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Recent upper respiratory infection", "Diffuse or migratory pain without classic migration to the right lower quadrant"], "exam": ["Tenderness may be less focal", "No peritoneal signs"], "labs": ["White count normal or mildly elevated"], "imaging": ["Enlarged mesenteric lymph nodes with a normal appendix on ultrasound or CT"]}}, {"name": "Crohn disease", "slug": "crohns-disease", "why_considered": "Right lower quadrant pain from terminal ileitis can mimic appendicitis.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Weeks to months of diarrhea", "Weight loss", "Prior similar episodes", "Family history of inflammatory bowel disease"], "exam": ["Right lower quadrant tenderness without acute peritoneal signs", "Possible perianal findings"], "labs": ["Anemia", "Elevated inflammatory markers", "White count may be normal"], "imaging": ["Terminal ileum wall thickening and inflammation on CT or MRI enterography"]}}, {"name": "Diverticulitis", "slug": "diverticulitis", "why_considered": "Left or lower abdominal pain with fever and leukocytosis; can present on the right in some patients.", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Older adult", "Left lower quadrant pain more typical", "Prior episodes of diverticulitis", "Constipation or change in bowel habit"], "exam": ["Left lower quadrant tenderness", "Possible palpable mass"], "labs": ["Leukocytosis", "Elevated CRP"], "imaging": ["Diverticula with pericolic fat stranding on CT"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Early, uncomplicated appendicitis: less than 24 hours of symptoms, localized right lower quadrant tenderness, low-grade or no fever, mild leukocytosis.", "moderate": "Established, uncomplicated appendicitis: clear migration of pain, guarding, fever, and marked leukocytosis.", "severe": "Perforated appendicitis with a localized abscess or phlegmon: high fever, persistent pain, palpable mass, and marked leukocytosis.", "critical": "Free perforation with generalized peritonitis and sepsis: rigid abdomen, hypotension, tachycardia, and altered mental status."}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Diffuse rigidity or rebound tenderness", "meaning": "Generalized peritonitis; needs urgent surgical evaluation."}, {"finding": "Hypotension or heart rate over 120", "meaning": "Sepsis; resuscitate and involve surgery immediately."}, {"finding": "Sudden relief of pain followed by worse, spreading pain", "meaning": "Perforation."}, {"finding": "Positive pregnancy test", "meaning": "Ectopic pregnancy until proven otherwise."}, {"finding": "Altered mental status or lethargy", "meaning": "Severe infection or sepsis, especially in older adults."}], "complications": [{"name": "Perforation", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Symptoms over 48 hours", "High fever", "Diffuse tenderness", "Sudden worsening of pain"]}, {"name": "Periappendiceal abscess", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Palpable right lower quadrant mass", "Persistent fever", "Failure to improve after antibiotics"]}, {"name": "Generalized peritonitis and sepsis", "timing": "early", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Rigid abdomen", "Hypotension", "Tachycardia", "Altered mental status"]}, {"name": "Adhesive small bowel obstruction", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Prior appendectomy with new distension and vomiting", "Crampy abdominal pain", "Decreased bowel sounds"]}, {"name": "Surgical site infection", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Wound redness, warmth, or drainage after appendectomy", "Fever"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Younger children cannot describe pain migration and often present irritable, vomiting, and refusing to walk; perforation rates are higher because diagnosis is delayed. Ultrasound is first-line imaging."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Findings are often atypical and milder; fever may be low or absent and the white count can be near normal even with perforation. Perforation rates are higher."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Appendicitis is the most common non-obstetric surgical emergency in pregnancy. The growing uterus pushes the appendix upward, so pain may be in the right flank or upper right abdomen, and nausea and vomiting may be attributed to pregnancy. Ultrasound first, then MRI if nondiagnostic."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "A thicker abdominal wall makes the exam less reliable and imaging harder to interpret; pain may feel less localized and the exam less impressive than the underlying disease."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "May have blunted fever and leukocytosis and can progress rapidly to perforation; maintain a lower threshold for imaging."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "Where did the pain start, and has it moved since then?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Pain that began around the belly button and then settled in the right lower abdomen"}, {"question": "When did the pain start, and is it getting better or worse?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Gradual onset over hours, steadily worsening, usually less than 48 hours"}, {"question": "Is the pain constant or does it come and go?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Constant once it localizes, with no pain-free stretches"}, {"question": "Does anything make it worse, like walking, coughing, or going over bumps in the car?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Pain worse with movement or jarring, suggesting peritoneal irritation"}, {"question": "Does anything make it feel better?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Lying still with the knees bent"}, {"question": "Did the vomiting start before or after the pain began?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Vomiting after the pain; vomiting first points toward gastroenteritis"}, {"question": "Have you felt like eating?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Loss of appetite"}, {"question": "Have you had any diarrhea?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Absent or mild; profuse watery diarrhea argues against appendicitis"}, {"question": "Have you felt warm or had chills?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Low-grade fever"}, {"question": "Have you been able to pass gas or have a bowel movement?", "category": "associated", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Inability to pass gas or constipation"}, {"question": "Any burning with urination, blood in the urine, or pain in your side or back?", "category": "ros", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Absent; present suggests a urinary cause such as a stone or infection"}, {"question": "Could you be pregnant? When was your last period?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Excludes ectopic pregnancy in anyone of childbearing potential"}, {"question": "Any vaginal discharge, bleeding, or pain with intercourse?", "category": "sexual", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Absent; present suggests pelvic inflammatory disease or an ectopic pregnancy"}, {"question": "Have you had your appendix removed, or any other abdominal surgery?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "No prior appendectomy"}, {"question": "Has anyone in your family had appendicitis?", "category": "family", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Appendicitis in a parent or sibling"}, {"question": "Has anyone around you had the same stomach illness?", "category": "social", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Sick contacts, which would point toward gastroenteritis"}, {"question": "Has the pain suddenly gotten much worse or spread across your whole belly?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Sudden worsening or diffuse pain suggesting perforation and peritonitis"}], "accepted_names": ["Acute appendicitis", "Appendicitis"], "reasoning_points": ["Pain that migrates from the periumbilical area to the right lower quadrant", "Anorexia with vomiting that began after the pain", "Localized right lower quadrant tenderness with peritoneal signs such as guarding, rebound, or pain with coughing", "Low-grade fever and leukocytosis with neutrophil predominance", "Pregnancy excluded with a negative hCG before imaging", "Imaging showing a dilated, noncompressible appendix with surrounding inflammation", "Look-alikes considered and addressed, including ectopic pregnancy, ovarian torsion, pelvic inflammatory disease, gastroenteritis, kidney stone, mesenteric adenitis, and Crohn disease"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Teenager or young adult with the classic migration story", "Focal McBurney point tenderness", "Low-grade fever and leukocytosis", "CT or ultrasound confirms an inflamed appendix"], "medium": ["Retrocecal appendix with flank pain, a positive psoas sign, and little anterior tenderness", "Mild sterile pyuria on urinalysis that could mislead toward a urinary infection", "Woman of reproductive age with gynecologic look-alikes requiring a pregnancy test and pelvic exam", "Symptoms present for more than 48 hours with concern for perforation"], "hard": ["Older adult with mild pain, little or no fever, and a near-normal white count", "Pregnant patient with right flank or upper right quadrant pain and nausea attributed to pregnancy", "Toddler who is irritable, vomiting, and refusing to walk, unable to describe the pain", "Patient with obesity and a less impressive exam than the underlying disease", "Presentation after perforation with diffuse pain and a rigid abdomen"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
