r than carcinoma, so tissue diagnosis is essential."}]}, "cancer": {"screening": "The US Preventive Services Task Force recommends biennial screening mammography for women aged 40 to 74 years. Women at higher risk because of a known BRCA mutation, prior chest radiation, or a strong family history may begin earlier and use MRI in addition to mammography.", "pathology": "Most breast cancers are adenocarcinomas arising from the ductal or lobular epithelium. Ductal carcinoma in situ is non-invasive; invasive ductal carcinoma is the most common invasive type. Lobular carcinoma is more often bilateral. Inflammatory breast cancer and Paget disease of the nipple are less common variants. Receptor status (estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, HER2) and grade guide treatment.", "staging_concepts": "Staging uses the TNM system: tumor size and chest wall or skin involvement (T), regional lymph node involvement (N), and distant metastasis (M). Stages I through III are non-metastatic; stage IV is metastatic disease. Early-stage disease is often detected by screening before a palpable mass is present.", "metastatic_sites": ["bone", "liver", "lung", "brain", "distant lymph nodes"]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "When did you first notice the lump, and has it changed since then?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Gradual onset over weeks to months; persistent or slowly enlarging rather than coming and going"}, {"question": "Does the lump change with your menstrual cycle, or is it there all the time?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Constant and non-cyclical; fluctuation with periods points to benign fibrocystic change"}, {"question": "Is the lump painful?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Usually painless; mild non-cyclical pain is possible"}, {"question": "Have you noticed any change in the size or shape of that breast?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Asymmetry or contour change of one breast"}, {"question": "Any dimpling, puckering, or orange-peel appearance of the skin over the lump?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Skin dimpling or peau d'orange suggesting local invasion"}, {"question": "Has either nipple turned inward or changed in appearance?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "New nipple retraction or scaly, crusted nipple and areola"}, {"question": "Any nipple discharge? If so, from one breast or both, and what color?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Bloody or clear discharge from a single duct in one breast"}, {"question": "Have you felt any lump or fullness in your armpit or above your collarbone?", "category": "associated", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Axillary or supraclavicular nodal involvement"}, {"question": "Have you had an abnormal mammogram or been told you need more breast imaging?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Abnormal screening mammogram or BI-RADS 4/5 assessment prompting biopsy"}, {"question": "Have you ever had breast cancer or a benign breast biopsy, especially one showing atypical cells?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prior breast cancer or atypical hyperplasia, both raising risk"}, {"question": "Have you ever had radiation therapy to the chest or breast?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prior chest radiation, such as mantle radiation for Hodgkin lymphoma"}, {"question": "Has anyone in your family had breast, ovarian, pancreatic, or prostate cancer, or male breast cancer?", "category": "family", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "First-degree relatives with breast or ovarian cancer, male breast cancer, or a known BRCA1/BRCA2 mutation"}, {"question": "How old were you when your periods started, and have you gone through menopause?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Early menarche or late menopause, meaning longer estrogen exposure"}, {"question": "Have you ever been pregnant, and how old were you at your first full-term birth?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Nulliparity or first birth after age 30"}, {"question": "Have you used menopausal hormone therapy, and for how long?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Current or recent combined estrogen plus progestin therapy"}, {"question": "How much alcohol do you drink in a typical week?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Regular alcohol use, a modifiable risk factor"}, {"question": "Have you had any unintentional weight loss, new back or hip pain, or new shortness of breath?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Symptoms suggesting metastatic disease to bone, liver, or lung"}, {"question": "Could you be pregnant? When was your last menstrual period?", "category": "obstetric", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Pregnancy status, which must be known before imaging, biopsy, or systemic therapy"}], "accepted_names": ["Breast cancer", "Breast carcinoma", "Invasive ductal carcinoma", "Invasive breast cancer", "Ductal carcinoma of the breast", "Carcinoma of the breast", "IDC", "DCIS", "Ductal carcinoma in situ"], "reasoning_points": ["A persistent, painless, non-cyclical breast mass or an abnormal screening mammogram in a woman over 40", "Risk factors such as older age, family history, BRCA1/BRCA2 mutation, prior chest radiation, or prolonged estrogen exposure", "Examination findings of a firm, irregular, fixed mass, with or without skin dimpling, nipple retraction, or axillary lymphadenopathy", "Diagnostic mammography and ultrasound showing a spiculated mass or suspicious microcalcifications, followed by core needle biopsy for tissue diagnosis", "Receptor status (estrogen receptor, progestero